WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.879
In this problem, a given dump trucks carrying capacity at the time of its production was

00:00:05.879 --> 00:00:08.240
13,000 pounds.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:13.599
At a given point in the future, when loaded to a routine load of 12,000 pounds at a dump

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:20.440
site, the dump trucks thrust rod used to lift and unload the bed of the truck failed.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:28.359
The above case is an example of which type of steel failure, fatigue, expansion, deformation,

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:30.759
or deflection.

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:35.679
This is an example of a conceptual problem that we would not be able to use the reference

00:00:35.679 --> 00:00:37.840
manual to help us with.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.600
Instead, it is a case where we would need to understand what is happening in the case

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.760
provided and recognize the type of failure that occurred.

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:52.879
In this case, the problem states that the dump truck is routinely loaded to 12,000 pounds,

00:00:52.879 --> 00:00:57.640
which is less than its maximum carrying capacity of 13,000 pounds.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:02.920
The fact that this is the same capacity at which the thrust rod failed can be understood

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.719
to be an example of fatigue.

00:01:05.719 --> 00:01:12.960
Fatigue type failures are due to a reduction in strength due to cyclical loading.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.640
The problem does not provide any more detail as to what exactly happened to the thrust

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.519
rod, so it would be a stretch to guess that any of the other type of failures listed

00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:24.760
here were the cause.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.000
I think it is important to study questions like this simply to be aware of the fact that

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:32.719
some things are not going to be in the reference manual.

00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:36.960
This is the type of information that would have been covered in many introductory materials

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:43.160
classes and is probably why questions like these are fair game on the civil PE.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:49.680
While there aren't many conceptual questions on the exam, there are enough that there's

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:54.080
going to be a few here and there where you're simply going to have to either know the

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.840
material or you don't.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.640
It's certainly not possible to know everything, so with questions like these, sometimes

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:02.280
you just have to do your best.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:07.320
I realize this isn't the most optimistic of advice, but for certain problems try not to

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.920
let it get you down.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:12.680
Remember, you're not looking for a perfect score on this test, you're just looking to

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:13.280
pass it.
